@node Requirements
@subsection Requirements

@itemize
@item
@strong{Emacs} 29.1 or later
@item
@strong{mpv} for live playback tracking
@item
@strong{eve CLI} for transcription and rendering (see below)
@item
@strong{FFmpeg with libass} and @strong{fontconfig} (@samp{fc-match}) for burned-in captions;
both tools are provided by this project's devenv environment
@item
@strong{hydra} (optional) for the @samp{?} keybinding cheatsheet popup
@end itemize

@node Installing the CLI
@subsection Installing the CLI

The Emacs package first looks for an @samp{eve} executable on your @samp{PATH}.  If it
does not find one, and @samp{eve-cli-program} is still set to its default @samp{"eve"},
it falls back to @samp{scripts/run-cli.sh} inside this checkout.  That script
creates a local virtualenv and installs the pre-built wheel from @samp{cli/dist/}.

For standard in-Emacs use, that local bootstrap path means a separate global
@samp{eve} install is optional.  The fallback path assumes Python 3.12 or later is
available and that the package checkout is writable.  It reuses an existing
@samp{cli/.venv}; it does not automatically refresh that environment when a new wheel
is shipped.  After updating the package, refresh an existing CLI installation
using its original installation method.  In a devenv development checkout,
use the project's Python synchronization workflow rather than replacing its
managed environment with a runtime wheel installation.

If you want @samp{eve} on your @samp{PATH} for shell use, install it globally:

@example
pip install cli/dist/eve_cli-*.whl
# or with uv:
uv pip install cli/dist/eve_cli-*.whl
@end example

Verify it works:

@example
eve doctor --json
@end example

