@node Starting transcription from Emacs
@section Starting transcription from Emacs

Two commands launch transcription:

@itemize
@item
@samp{M-x eve-transcribe} prompts for a directory, scans for media files, and
starts @samp{eve transcribe} asynchronously.  While it runs, Eve shows a visible
@samp{Eve: transcribing…} mode-line status.  On success the manifest opens
automatically in eve-mode.

@item
@samp{M-x eve-dired-transcribe} runs from Dired using the marked files (or the
file at point when nothing is marked).
@end itemize

On failure, the @samp{*eve transcribe*} buffer appears so you can inspect the error
output.

@node Timing and output validity
@section Timing and output validity

The transcriber writes v1.1 with a per-source audio sample clock, canonical
spoken words, and tick intervals.  Backend precision is retained through
segmentation and converted once to nearest source-sample ticks.  The 16 kHz
ASR working audio preserves leading media-time offsets.

New manifests explicitly keep fillers and set @samp{render.preserve_short_gaps} to
@samp{0}.  Change these saved render settings in the raw JSON companion when needed;
they are authoritative for v1.1 rendering.

Missing, reversed, zero-duration, or sub-tick-collapsed word intervals block
publication of a successful transcription.  Eve does not add a tick or stretch
neighbouring words to make validation pass.  Existing output is left untouched
on validation failure.  Default transcription preserves ASR estimates without
certifying acoustic accuracy.  The CLI's opt-in @samp{--word-aligner ctc} path uses
unchanged native CTC estimates after English faster-whisper recognition; no human
timing annotations are needed.  Unsigned English integers, including grouped
thousands and sentence punctuation, use alignment-only verbalization.  @samp{11,}
remains the same token and spelling while CTC matches @samp{ELEVEN}.  Multiword
readings remain atomic.  Four-digit numbers compare cardinal and year readings
against the same acoustic emissions, rather than assuming a year pronunciation.
Selected readings and the pinned normalizer's version are saved in the alignment
audit.  Ambiguous score ties or more than 32 reading paths fail without guessing.
Decimals, signed/currency/percentage forms, leading zeros, numeric compounds and
ordinals remain unsupported.

Experimental backend status does not require force.  A recognizer/CTC boundary
disagreement greater than 160 ms is recorded in @samp{alignment.diagnostics} without
blocking publication: disagreement alone does not establish that CTC is wrong.
Missing or invalid recognizer timing used only for comparison is also a
non-blocking @samp{recognizer_timing_unavailable} diagnostic when independent native
alignment passes structural validation.  The report retains the original
comparison boundaries as strings, or null for missing values, including
non-finite values without invalid JSON@.  This does not repair either estimate
or certify word presence or timing accuracy.  Without an independent aligner,
invalid recognizer word timing remains authoritative and blocks publication.
@samp{--force} may accept other recorded quality concerns, never invalid native intervals,
changed tokens, source-bound violations or model errors.  Informational
diagnostics alone never set the recorded @samp{forced} flag, even with @samp{--force}.
Selected media envelopes are retained rather than shrunk to character spans.
If expanded caption envelopes intersect, they are partitioned jointly between
adjacent word intervals, retaining exactly the same source coverage without
repeating footage.  Non-overlapping captions and intended gaps stay unchanged.
Word estimates, grouping and order do not change; genuinely overlapping word
timings still fail instead of being repaired.

Alignment writes a decision report beside the output (or at
@samp{--alignment-report PATH}).  Successful manifests store the same @samp{alignment}
audit record, including model/revision, concerns and any actual override.
@samp{eve text-edit ... --report render.json} carries that record into its render
report.  Subtitle settings still live in TJM@.

In Emacs, set @samp{eve-transcribe-word-aligner} to @samp{"ctc"} to opt into this path
for @samp{M-x eve-transcribe} and @samp{M-x eve-dired-transcribe}.  The default @samp{nil}
keeps backend word timings.  The CTC path requires English @samp{faster-whisper}
and the CLI's declared @samp{alignment} dependencies.  Its progress shows the word
alignment stage, and success opens the manifest as usual.

A normal run never passes @samp{--force}.  Use @samp{C-u M-x eve-transcribe} or
@samp{C-u M-x eve-dired-transcribe} to request a quality override for that run;
this requires the CTC opt-in and still cannot override invalid timing.
A failure shows @samp{*eve transcribe*} and is never retried automatically.
The alignment audit, including false booleans and empty arrays, survives
editor saving and undo.  Empty segment tag lists also remain arrays in saved
and render manifests.  CTC completion skips legacy suspicious-region ASR
repair offers; the word-aligner setting does not change manual region
retranscription commands.

The pause diagnostic is provisional and non-blocking: Silero-VAD gaps of at
least 250 ms, with 80 ms excluded at each edge.  Representative validation is
still needed before it becomes a blocking gate.  Runtime completion does not
certify the separate release target of 95% of reference boundaries within 80 ms
(40 ms stretch), nor the operational target of 95% normal completion.

@node Backends
@section Backends

@samp{eve transcribe} supports three backends:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Backend
@tab Flag
@tab Notes
@item @samp{faster-whisper}
@tab @samp{--backend faster-whisper}
@tab Default.  Fast, local inference.
@item @samp{transformers}
@tab @samp{--backend transformers}
@tab HuggingFace models.  Needs optional deps.
@item @samp{nemo}
@tab @samp{--backend nemo}
@tab NVIDIA NeMo.  Needs optional deps.
@end multitable

The @samp{--verbatim} flag (on by default in Emacs) biases @samp{faster-whisper} toward
preserving disfluencies and filler words.  In verbatim mode, faster-whisper also
receives default filler hotwords such as @samp{um}, @samp{uh}, @samp{like}, and @samp{you know};
set @samp{eve-transcribe-hotwords} or pass @samp{--hotwords} to override them.  For
@samp{transformers} and @samp{nemo}, choose a verbatim-friendly model instead.

For an explicitly supplied local CTranslate2 model, the CLI also supports an
experimental @samp{--recognition-profile trained-verbatim} mode:

@example
eve transcribe input.mp4 --output edit.tjm.json \
  --recognition-profile trained-verbatim --model /path/local-ct2-model \
  --device cpu --word-aligner ctc
@end example

This fixes English CPU/int8 decoding at beam 5, with VAD, temperature zero,
no previous-text conditioning, and no initial prompt or hotwords.  It does not
change defaults, download or convert recognition weights, or certify quality.  The local
directory must contain @samp{model.bin}, @samp{config.json}, @samp{tokenizer.json} and
@samp{preprocessor_config.json} and @samp{vocabulary.json}.  Actual file hashes and the experimental profile
are recorded in @samp{alignment.recognition}; hashes do not certify upstream provenance.
The existing native aligner retains its own pinned model/cache and offline policy;
set @samp{HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1} to forbid model downloads.

Unchanged recognizer text supplies whole-word identity: @samp{I'M} is one timed token,
even when the SDK fragments it into @samp{I} and @samp{'M}.  Exact character mapping is
required; ambiguous mapping fails rather than correcting text.  Native CTC
independently estimates the whole-word interval; fragment timings are never
stitched.  Original SDK text, fragment boundaries and mappings are retained in
@samp{alignment.sources[].recognition_segments}.  Caption splitting occurs afterward,
without changing word times or losing selected media/gaps.  Existing outputs
survive failures.  See @uref{../cli/README.md, CLI documentation} for the tested candidate and revision.

For full-source Emacs commands, including @samp{eve-dired-transcribe}, select the
recognition profile independently of the backend and model:

@lisp
(let ((eve-transcribe-backend "faster-whisper")
      (eve-transcribe-model "/path/local-ct2-model")
      (eve-transcribe-recognition-profile "trained-verbatim")
      (eve-transcribe-word-aligner "ctc")
      (eve-transcribe-verbatim t)
      (eve-transcribe-hotwords nil))
  (call-interactively #'eve-dired-transcribe))
@end lisp

Alternatively, customize those variables for subsequent runs.  The profile does
not select a checkpoint: another compatible local model needs only a different
@samp{eve-transcribe-model} value, not a new model-specific mode or registry entry.
A different backend/decoding policy still needs explicit CLI/frontend support
and separate quality evidence.  Unsupported combinations fail before launch;
there is no automatic model switch, fallback or force.  The selected profile is
captured per job and recorded by the CLI in the alignment audit.  The default
profile keeps existing command behavior.  Non-default profiles are not supported
for region retranscription and are rejected rather than silently using another
policy.  Literal casing and contractions are preserved; display rewriting is
never a required or automatic transcription step.

Choose recognition and alignment models whose published licenses permit the
intended use of both their weights and generated outputs.  This is a
non-commercial project; non-commercial research models may be evaluated here.
Check model and output terms, not only the inference code's license, and verify
production or operational deployment rights separately before routine use.

Licensing eligibility and backend availability do not establish verbatim recall
or timing accuracy.  Model quality must be qualified separately; a backend's
@samp{--verbatim} option or a model's name is not a guarantee of filler preservation.

