@node Deletion and restoration
@section Deletion and restoration

@itemize
@item
@samp{d} (@samp{eve-delete-word}) — marks the word at point as deleted.  Deleted
words are hidden in the buffer (@samp{eve-hide-deleted-mode}) and excluded from
compilation.  The operation is non-destructive: the word remains in the JSON
and can be restored.

@item
@samp{D} (@samp{eve-delete-segment}) — marks the entire current segment as deleted.
@end itemize

Deleted content is shown or hidden with @samp{C-c C-h} (@samp{eve-hide-deleted-mode}).

@node Splitting and merging
@section Splitting and merging

@itemize
@item
@samp{s} or @samp{|} (@samp{eve-split-segment}) — splits the current segment at point,
creating two segments with the text and timing divided at the cut.
In the segment panel, the word under point takes precedence over older
keyboard focus.  Modal keymaps can override @samp{s}; @samp{|} or
@samp{M-x eve-segment-panel-split} invokes the panel split command directly.

@item
@samp{m} or @samp{)} (@samp{eve-merge-with-next}) — merges the current segment with the
one below, combining text and adjusting the end time.
@end itemize

For v1.1, splitting partitions the original source interval at an existing
word edge without retiming words or losing retained footage.  Merging preserves
word records and refreshes the combined caption.  Different source clocks,
backwards/overlapping source intervals, and mixed timed/untimed captions cannot
be forced into one timed group.  Reordering remains valid without changing any
source-local ticks.

@strong{Regroup first, then delete:} v1.1 split/merge commands reject groups with active
word cuts, including fillers removed by @samp{render.filler_policy = "drop"}.  Restore
the cuts before regrouping, then reapply them.  A filler mark with policy
@samp{"keep"} is not an active cut; explicit non-filler decisions remain respected.
Merging also refuses to span a deleted group, including one hidden between the
selected groups.  Force cannot bypass these checks.  This prevents the
renderer’s per-group trimming rules from silently reinstating omitted footage;
rejection leaves both data and undo history unchanged.

@node Reordering
@section Reordering

@itemize
@item
@samp{J} (@samp{eve-move-segment-down}) — move the current segment one position down.
@item
@samp{K} (@samp{eve-move-segment-up}) — move the current segment one position up.
@item
@samp{y} (@samp{eve-copy-segment}) — copy the current segment or marker, including edit
metadata such as b-roll.
@item
@samp{C-y} (@samp{eve-paste-segment}) — paste the copied segment after point.  Pasting
into another TJM buffer also copies the referenced source descriptor when
needed.
@end itemize

@node Markers and sections
@section Markers and sections

Press @samp{o} (@samp{eve-insert-marker}) to insert a marker above the current segment.
Markers act as named section headings.  They appear in the buffer as bold
titles.

When you compile with @samp{C-c C-c} while point is on a marker, the marker and the
segments in that section are compiled.  Plain markers are structural headings and
render no media, but a marker with @samp{edit.broll} is included as a timeline unit.
This is useful for intro cards, outro cards, interstitial title cards, and for
working on one chapter of a longer video.

@node Inline editing
@section Inline editing

Press @samp{e} (@samp{eve-dwim-edit}) to edit the text or metadata of the segment at
point.  On a marker it edits the title.  For timed v1.1 segments it changes
@samp{display} fragments, never the factual @samp{spoken} tokens or their source ticks.
The panel's @samp{i} action edits one focused token or the selected token range;
@samp{E} edits the segment's token displays.

A single v1.1 token can display a complete phrase, which highlights and deletes
as one unit.  For a bulk replacement, supply one replacement word per selected
timed token; otherwise edit the tokens individually.  Ambiguous replacements
are rejected before changing data or undo history.  Capitalization and
punctuation actions also change only token displays.  Karaoke caption text is
kept synchronized with surviving token displays after word edits.

The legacy seconds-only time editor and local transcript regeneration are not
available for v1.1: they fail without changing data rather than silently
rewriting aliases or losing exact clocks.  Explicit source-tick changes can be
made through the raw JSON view.  Existing-timeline editing and export do not
require transcription or timing review.

@node Metadata
@section Metadata

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@tab Description
@item @samp{t}
@tab @samp{eve-toggle-tag}
@tab Toggle a tag on the segment at point.
@item @samp{i}
@tab @samp{eve-edit-speaker}
@tab Set the speaker label for the segment.
@item @samp{r}
@tab @samp{eve-edit-start-end}
@tab Edit legacy v1 timecodes; rejected safely for v1.1.
@item @samp{C-c C-n}
@tab @samp{eve-edit-notes}
@tab Edit free-form notes for the segment.
@end multitable

@node Undo and redo
@section Undo and redo

@itemize
@item
@samp{u} (@samp{eve-undo}) — undo the last edit.
@item
@samp{C-r} (@samp{eve-redo}) — redo a previously undone edit.
@end itemize

Eve maintains its own undo stack separate from Emacs's built-in undo.

@node Validation
@section Validation

Eve validates the manifest automatically on every save (controlled by
@samp{eve-validation-on-save}).  Manual validation runs with @samp{C-c C-v}
(@samp{eve-validate}).  Timing inconsistencies are reported in the echo area.

@node Filler Words
@chapter Filler Words

@menu
* Concept::
* Configuration: Configuration (1). 
* Commands::
* Auto-tagging on file open::
@end menu

@node Concept
@section Concept

Filler words ("um", "uh", "you know") clutter transcripts.  Eve's filler
workflow is non-destructive: words are @emph{marked} as fillers first, then
@emph{optionally deleted}.  Marked fillers are highlighted in the buffer with a
distinct face and excluded from compilation.  You can mark a word as non-filler
for this TJM file so future auto-tagging leaves it alone.

@node Configuration (1)
@section Configuration

@lisp
;; Plain strings — single words or multi-word phrases
(setq eve-filler-phrases '("um" "uh" "you know" "to be honest with you"))
@end lisp

@samp{eve-filler-phrases} stores phrases as plain strings.  Matching is
case-insensitive, punctuation-insensitive, and prefers the longest phrase first.
For example, @samp{"you know"} matches adjacent words like @samp{you know,} and marks both
words as a filler phrase; it does not mark separated words such as @samp{you ...
know}.  Eve stores editable filler decisions in @samp{word.edit.filler}; legacy
@samp{word.edit.kind = "filler"} metadata is still recognized.
The list is persisted when you use the interactive add commands.

@samp{eve-filler-exception-phrases} stores phrases that should not be auto-marked as
fillers.  Exceptions win over filler phrases: if @samp{like} is a filler but
@samp{would like} is an exception, the @samp{like} in @samp{would like} stays non-filler.

The legacy @samp{eve-filler-regex} variable is still honoured for backwards
compatibility but @samp{eve-filler-phrases} is preferred.

