@node Auto-tagging on file open
@section Auto-tagging on file open

Fillers are tagged automatically every time a TJM manifest is opened.  If you
add a new phrase to @samp{eve-filler-phrases}, reopen the file or run
@samp{M-x eve-tag-fillers} to apply the updated list.  Words explicitly marked as
non-filler with @samp{A} are persisted in the TJM file and are not auto-tagged again.

@node B-Roll
@chapter B-Roll

@menu
* Concept: Concept (1). 
* Editing b-roll metadata::
* Compositing modes in detail::
* Audio policy::
* JSON templates::
* Placeholders without templates::
* Continuation chains::
@end menu

@node Concept (1)
@section Concept

B-roll is supplementary footage that plays over (or instead of) the main
recording.  Eve supports three compositing modes:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Replace} (default) — the b-roll clip completely replaces the source video
for the duration of the segment.  The source audio is kept unless you change
the audio policy.

@item
@strong{Pip} (picture-in-picture) — the b-roll is scaled down and composited on
top of the source video.  You control position and size.

@item
@strong{Overlay} — alias for replace.  Treated identically by the renderer.
@end itemize

@node Editing b-roll metadata
@section Editing b-roll metadata

Press @samp{b} (@samp{eve-edit-broll}) on a segment to set or modify its b-roll.  The
command prompts for each field in turn:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Field
@tab Prompt
@tab Description
@item @samp{file}
@tab "B-roll file"
@tab Path to a video, image, or @samp{.json} template.  Blank clears the b-roll.
@item @samp{mode}
@tab "Mode (overlay/replace/pip)"
@tab Compositing mode (see above).
@item @samp{audio}
@tab "Audio (source/mute/broll)"
@tab Which audio track the rendered segment uses.
@item @samp{start_offset}
@tab "Start offset (MM:SS or seconds)"
@tab Where to begin playback in the b-roll clip.
@item @samp{duration}
@tab "Duration (MM:SS or seconds)"
@tab How long the b-roll plays; blank uses the segment duration.
@item @samp{still}
@tab "Treat as still image?"
@tab Auto-detected for @samp{.png}, @samp{.jpg}, etc.  Loops the image for the segment.
@item @samp{continue}
@tab "Continue from previous?"
@tab Part of a continuation chain (see below).
@end multitable

Additional keys:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@tab Description
@item @samp{B}
@tab @samp{eve-toggle-broll-continue}
@tab Copy b-roll from the previous segment and mark as continuation.
@item @samp{C-c C-b}
@tab @samp{eve-edit-broll-placeholders}
@tab Edit placeholder values for the current segment.
@end multitable

B-roll metadata is stored in the segment's @samp{edit.broll} field in the JSON
manifest.

@node Compositing modes in detail
@section Compositing modes in detail

@menu
* Replace mode::
* Pip mode::
@end menu

@node Replace mode
@subsection Replace mode

The b-roll video replaces the source video entirely.  The renderer trims the
b-roll to the segment's duration, starting at @samp{start_offset}.  If the b-roll
is shorter than the segment, it is extended to fit.

When @samp{still} is true, the image is looped for the full segment duration using
@samp{ffmpeg -loop 1}.

@node Pip mode
@subsection Pip mode

The b-roll is scaled and positioned as an inset window over the source.
Position and size are controlled by a @samp{position} object in the JSON:

@example
@{
  "file": "demo-recording.mp4",
  "mode": "pip",
  "position": @{
    "x": 0.05,
    "y": 0.05,
    "width": 0.3
  @}
@}
@end example

@multitable {aaaaaaa} {aaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Field
@tab Default
@tab Description
@item @samp{x}
@tab @samp{0.05}
@tab Horizontal offset as a fraction of the main video width (0.0--1.0).
@item @samp{y}
@tab @samp{0.05}
@tab Vertical offset as a fraction of the main video height (0.0--1.0).
@item @samp{width}
@tab @samp{0.3}
@tab Width of the pip window as a fraction of the main video width.
@end multitable

The height is calculated automatically to preserve the b-roll's aspect ratio.

@node Audio policy
@section Audio policy

Each b-roll entry has an @samp{audio} field that controls which audio track
appears in the rendered segment:

@itemize
@item
@samp{source} (default) — keep the original recording's audio.
@item
@samp{broll} — use the audio track from the b-roll clip.  Not available for
still images.
@item
@samp{mute} — no audio for this segment.
@end itemize

@node JSON templates
@section JSON templates

When the @samp{file} field points to a @samp{.json} file instead of a media file, eve
treats it as a @strong{template}.  The template JSON has this structure:

@example
@{
  "template": "lower-third.png",
  "overlays": [
    @{
      "placeholder": "SPEAKER_NAME",
      "fontsize": 36,
      "color": "white",
      "x": 80,
      "y": 620,
      "boxColor": "black@@0.6"
    @},
    @{
      "placeholder": "SPEAKER_TITLE",
      "fontsize": 24,
      "color": "#cccccc",
      "x": 80,
      "y": 665
    @}
  ],
  "placeholders": @{
    "SPEAKER_NAME": "Full Name",
    "SPEAKER_TITLE": "Job Title"
  @}
@}
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{template} — path to the actual media file (image or video).
@item
@samp{overlays} — a list of text overlays rendered with ffmpeg @samp{drawtext}.
Each overlay references a @samp{placeholder} key by name.
@item
@samp{placeholders} — default values for each placeholder.  Segment-level
overrides (set via @samp{C-c C-b}) take precedence.
@end itemize

@menu
* Overlay fields::
* Concrete example lower-third title card::
@end menu

@node Overlay fields
@subsection Overlay fields

Each overlay in the @samp{overlays} array supports:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Field
@tab Description
@item @samp{placeholder}
@tab Name of the placeholder key this overlay renders.
@item @samp{fontsize} (or @samp{size})
@tab Font size in pixels.
@item @samp{color} (or @samp{fontColor})
@tab Font colour (ffmpeg colour syntax: name, @samp{#rrggbb}, @samp{white@@0.8}).
@item @samp{x} (or @samp{position_x})
@tab Horizontal position (pixels or ffmpeg expression like @samp{(w-text_w)/2}).
@item @samp{y} (or @samp{position_y})
@tab Vertical position (pixels or ffmpeg expression).
@item @samp{align}
@tab Set to @samp{"center"} to auto-centre horizontally and vertically.
@item @samp{font} (or @samp{fontfile})
@tab Path to a @samp{.ttf} or @samp{.otf} font file.
@item @samp{boxColor} (or @samp{box_color})
@tab Background box colour (e.g. @samp{black@@0.6} for 60% opacity).
@item @samp{shadowColor} / @samp{shadow_x} / @samp{shadow_y}
@tab Drop shadow parameters.
@item @samp{start} / @samp{end} / @samp{duration}
@tab Time window (seconds) during which the overlay is visible.
@end multitable

@node Concrete example lower-third title card
@subsection Concrete example: lower-third title card

Suppose you have a background image @samp{lower-third.png} and you want to show
the speaker's name and title during a segment.

@enumerate
@item
Create the template file @samp{assets/lower-third.json}:

@example
@{
  "template": "assets/lower-third.png",
  "overlays": [
    @{
      "placeholder": "NAME",
      "fontsize": 42,
      "color": "white",
      "x": 80,
      "y": 620,
      "font": "assets/Inter-Bold.ttf",
      "boxColor": "black@@0.5"
    @},
    @{
      "placeholder": "ROLE",
      "fontsize": 28,
      "color": "#cccccc",
      "x": 80,
      "y": 672,
      "font": "assets/Inter-Regular.ttf"
    @}
  ],
  "placeholders": @{
    "NAME": "Speaker Name",
    "ROLE": "Speaker Role"
  @}
@}
@end example

@item
In eve-mode, press @samp{b} on the segment where you want the title card.
Set @samp{file} to @samp{assets/lower-third.json}.  Set @samp{mode} to @samp{replace}.

@item
Press @samp{C-c C-b} to edit placeholders for this segment:

@example
Placeholder NAME (default Speaker Name): Jane Doe
Placeholder ROLE (default Speaker Role): Product Lead
New placeholder key (blank to finish):
@end example

@item
Compile with @samp{C-c C-c}.  The rendered segment shows @samp{lower-third.png}
with "Jane Doe" and "Product Lead" burned in as text overlays at the
specified positions and sizes.
@end enumerate

Each segment can override different placeholder values from the same
template.  The template is reused; only the text changes.

@node Placeholders without templates
@section Placeholders without templates

You can also set placeholder key-value pairs directly on a segment without
a JSON template, using @samp{C-c C-b}.  These are stored in the manifest under
@samp{edit.broll.placeholders} and are available for any overlay definitions.

@node Continuation chains
@section Continuation chains

When a single b-roll clip should span multiple consecutive segments, use
continuation chains:

@enumerate
@item
Press @samp{b} on the first segment and configure the b-roll normally.
@item
Move to the next segment and press @samp{B} (@samp{eve-toggle-broll-continue}).
@end enumerate

@samp{B} copies the b-roll metadata from the nearest preceding segment that has
b-roll, then auto-calculates:

@itemize
@item
@samp{continue}: set to @samp{true}.
@item
@samp{start_offset}: previous offset + previous segment duration, so playback
continues seamlessly from where the last segment left off.
@item
@samp{duration}: remaining duration of the b-roll clip (if the original had a
duration set).
@end itemize

Repeat @samp{B} on each subsequent segment in the chain.  The renderer treats the
entire chain as one continuous b-roll playback — the clip advances smoothly
across segment boundaries without restarting.

@node Compilation and Rendering
@chapter Compilation and Rendering

@menu
* Two modes draft and final::
* Burned-in captions::
