* Pre-flight validation::
* Dry-run analysis::
* Checkpoint and resume::
* Progressive output::
* Configuration reference::
* CLI reference::
@end menu

@node Two modes draft and final
@section Two modes: draft and final

@samp{C-c C-c} (@samp{eve-compile}) compiles the manifest at draft quality — fast
enough to catch mistakes before committing to a full export.

@samp{C-u C-c C-c} compiles at final quality (H.264 medium/CRF 18) for delivery.

The quality used can be changed permanently with @samp{eve-compile-quality}.

@node Burned-in captions
@section Burned-in captions

An existing valid v1.1 timeline can be opened and exported directly; no new
transcription or alignment is required.  Eve reads source-local ticks using
each source's timebase, even without legacy @samp{start}, @samp{end}, @samp{text} or @samp{token}
aliases.  Displayed seconds are a view: opening, configuring subtitles and
saving do not round or replace the authoritative ticks.  Existing @samp{word.display}
fragments appear together as one timed token, including multiword fragments;
spoken text remains separate.

Run @samp{M-x eve-enable-subtitle-burning} in a v1.1 TJM buffer or its context panel,
then save with @samp{C-x C-s} or compile normally.  The command copies
@samp{eve-subtitle-defaults} into the manifest's optional @samp{subtitles} object; the
resulting video follows those saved settings, not the current Emacs session.
Existing subtitle settings are left unchanged, including extension fields.
Legacy v1 does not support burning: supply a valid v1.1 timeline instead.
Changing only the version field does not add the required clock/tick data.

The defaults use word highlighting, bold 144-pixel white text, a 3-pixel black
outline, and a gold active token.  Captions are bottom-centered with a 180-pixel
margin, scaled from a 1920×1080 reference canvas.  The large text-first profile
is intentional: speech and captions can dominate the video rather than serve
as a small annotation.  On a 1080×1920 portrait export, uniform scaling gives
an 81-pixel font and a 101.25-pixel bottom margin.  One TJM segment is one caption;
pauses leave the caption visible without highlighting, and captions disappear
between segments.

The requested font must be installed.  For example, to use DejaVu Sans for
manifests that do not yet have subtitle settings:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'eve
  (setf (alist-get 'family (alist-get 'font eve-subtitle-defaults))
        "DejaVu Sans"))
@end lisp

Customize the complete default profile with @samp{M-x customize-variable RET
eve-subtitle-defaults RET}.  Its values are JSON-compatible alists: @samp{t} is true,
@samp{:json-false} is false, and @samp{nil} is null.  To use plain captions instead of
highlighting, set its @samp{style} member to @samp{"plain"}.

Run @samp{M-x eve-disable-subtitle-burning} to remove the manifest's subtitle object.
Enabling/disabling is undoable with Eve's normal undo command.  Changing Emacs
defaults never replaces settings already saved in a TJM file; to deliberately
replace them, disable burning and then enable it again with the new defaults.
These commands do not change media selections, token timings, or optional
embedded subtitle tracks.  Burning re-encodes video; audio timing is preserved.

Fractional cuts retain native frame phase, including at 29.97 fps.  The first
video frame may legitimately have a nonzero presentation time when the source
cut falls between frames.  Encoding, embedded-track muxing and burning preserve
that phase rather than rounding it onto a new frame-rate grid.  Clock-rule
changes invalidate older render caches and resume checkpoints automatically.

Compilation output appears once in the bottom context view when context-panels
supports buffer adoption, or in one standalone bottom window otherwise.
Repeated renders keep one log view and preserve focus in the source buffer.
Render validation failures appear in that log without automatically opening
Python source or the CLI launcher.  Invalid token timings identify the
segment, token, and interval that need repair; missing fonts must be installed
or replaced in the manifest settings.  Eve does not invent timing or silently
substitute fonts to make a render succeed.

@node Under the hood
@section Under the hood

Eve's rendering pipeline is designed to be fast after the first run:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Quality profiles} — draft uses MJPEG encoding at ultrafast preset; final
uses H.264 at medium preset.  The codec and preset are resolved
automatically from the @samp{--quality} flag.

@item
@strong{Parallel encoding} — segments are encoded concurrently across all available
CPU cores (@samp{eve-compile-jobs}).

@item
@strong{Segment cache} — rendered segments are stored in @samp{.eve-cache/} next to the
manifest, keyed by a SHA-256 hash of the segment's content and encoding
settings.  Unchanged segments are reused; only changed ones re-encode.

@item
@strong{Speculative pre-rendering} — when @samp{eve-auto-render-mode} is enabled, after
each save an idle timer (@samp{eve-auto-render-idle-seconds}) fires a low-priority
background render of the segments that changed.  When you press @samp{C-c C-c},
the cache is already warm and compilation reduces to a concat step.  Use
@samp{M-x eve-render-cache-changed} to warm changed segments immediately, or
@samp{M-x eve-render-cache-all} to proactively warm every segment cache entry.

@item
@strong{Context thumbnails} — timestamp rows in the optional context side panel can
show cached thumbnails from the rendered output when it already exists and is
fresh, falling back to source media frames.  Thumbnail extraction is lazy,
visible-row-driven, low-priority, and never triggers a full render.
@end itemize

@node Pre-flight validation
@section Pre-flight validation

Before starting a render, eve validates the manifest:

@itemize
@item
All source files must exist and be readable.
@item
All b-roll files must exist.
@item
Segment timecodes must be non-negative and end after start.
@item
Words must fall within their segment bounds.
@end itemize

Warnings appear in the echo area.  Errors block compilation unless you
confirm.  Run validation explicitly with @samp{C-c C-v} (@samp{eve-validate}).

If the estimated render time (from the dry-run analysis) exceeds
@samp{eve-compile-confirm-threshold} seconds, eve asks for confirmation.

@node Dry-run analysis
@section Dry-run analysis

Before rendering, eve can report which segments would be re-encoded and
estimate the render time:

@example
eve text-edit manifest.tjm.json --output out.mp4 --dry-run
@end example

The output (JSON with @samp{--json}) includes @samp{total_segments},
@samp{changed_segments}, and @samp{estimated_seconds}.  This information feeds the
pre-flight confirmation prompt.

@node Checkpoint and resume
@section Checkpoint and resume

If a render is interrupted, eve writes a checkpoint file in @samp{.eve-cache/} as
segments complete.  The next run picks up from where it left off: segments
already in the cache are reused, and only the remaining ones are encoded.

Pass @samp{--no-resume} to force a fresh render from scratch.

@node Progressive output
@section Progressive output

With @samp{--partial-every N}, eve writes a partial @samp{<output>.partial.mp4} after
every N segments complete.  A JSON event is emitted on stdout for each
partial file, which Emacs can use to update a live preview.

@node Configuration reference
@section Configuration reference

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Variable
@tab Default
@tab Description
@item @samp{eve-compile-quality}
@tab @samp{"draft"}
@tab @samp{"draft"} for fast preview, @samp{"final"} for delivery.
@item @samp{eve-compile-jobs}
@tab @samp{0}
@tab Parallel encode workers; 0 = auto-detect CPU count.
@item @samp{eve-compile-scale}
@tab @samp{1.0}
@tab Resolution scale for draft (e.g. @samp{0.5} = half res).
@item @samp{eve-compile-cache}
@tab @samp{t}
@tab Use the @samp{.eve-cache/} segment cache.
@item @samp{eve-compile-confirm-threshold}
@tab @samp{30}
